Q: How do you check if a JavaScript Object is a DOM Object? A: <p>Answers that use <code>instanceof</code> work well, but keep in mind that they don't work if you are testing values that might originate from a different frame or window.</p>
<p>For that one can test for a DOM node with <code>document.cloneNode.call(obj, 0)</code> which throws only when passed a value that isn't a DOM node. So it's just a matter of using try/catch.</p>
<p>Unlike <code>instanceof</code>, <code>document.cloneNode.call(obj, 0)</code> also works in old browsers. The exception is Internet Explorer 8 and below where one must use a different approach. Thankfully it is just a matter of trying to write to <code>obj.nodeType</code> as that throws only if it's a DOM node. So again it's just a matter of using try/catch.</p>
<p>So here is the complete function. This works in various old browsers including IE6+:</p>
<pre><code>// Cross browser solution that works with high accuracy
function isDOMNode(obj) {
    'use strict';
    var use_ie_test = this &amp;&amp; this!==this.self;// dirty test for IE 6-8
    var result;
    try {
        use_ie_test?
            obj &amp;&amp; {}.hasOwnProperty.call(obj, 'nodeType') &amp;&amp; (obj.nodeType = obj.nodeType):// throws if node
            result = document.cloneNode.call(obj, 0);// throws if not a node
    }
    catch(e) {
        result = use_ie_test;
    }
    return !!result;// cast to boolean
}
</code></pre>
<p>From there it's very easy to find out exactly what kind of node it is by checking the nodeType and tagName values.</p>
